From 88b119bc3120702d4437046a5cca15a3084e86dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fountain5405 <Fountain5405@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 04:43:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 9/11] blockchain_db: warm_indexes(), reading the hash indexes
 into the page cache in a thread of its own

A new virtual, BlockchainDB::warm_indexes(), does nothing by default.
BlockchainLMDB's version starts a thread and returns. The thread reads
three tables with mdb_env_warm_dbis(): spent_keys, tx_indices and
block_heights, in one read transaction. Every block's sync looks these up
at random places. On a spinning disk, each lookup that misses the page
cache costs a seek.

- The levels above the leaves are always read: about 2% of the pages,
  16 MB on the stressnet chain. With them in the cache, each lookup costs
  one read instead of about four.
- The leaves are read only if all of them fit in 60% of the memory this
  process may use. That is the physical memory, lowered by a cgroup limit:
  v2 for this process's group and each group above it, and v1. On the
  stressnet chain the leaves are 0.81 GB, so a 2 GB machine reads them and
  a 1 GB one does not. If the memory size is unknown, the leaves are not
  read.
- One log line at the end reports what was read and how long it took, or
  why the leaves were skipped. A line when the thread starts says that it
  reads in the background.
- Any failure is logged as a warning, and the node goes on without the
  warm-up.

The thread holds a read transaction for as long as it reads, and reads
pages through the map. So BlockchainLMDB stops it, and waits for it, in
two places:
- close(), before the environment and its map go;
- do_resize(), before prevent_new_txns(). A resize waits for every read
  transaction to end and holds up new ones meanwhile, so it would have
  waited for the whole warm-up; and the warm-up could itself be waiting
  at that gate to begin its transaction. A resize ends the warm-up; it
  is not started again.
It stops within one page or one read-ahead request (through the stop
function of mdb_env_warm_dbis()), and logs what it had read.

Measured in the first, synchronous version of the warm-up, on a test VM
(4 vCPUs, 4 GB RAM) on a 5,400 rpm disk with the uncompacted stressnet DB
at 3,106,400: the three indexes took 61 s this way, against 2,054 s in key
order. With the same pages resident, a 400-block sync took 83 s instead of
360 s cold.

Measured on the stressnet DB in the background version, on a 12-core Ryzen
9 3900X (16 GB RAM, SATA SSD), data.mdb out of the page cache, reads
limited to 15 MB/s by a cgroup as a stand-in for a slow disk, so that the
warm-up takes 55 s: two seconds into a warm-up, a resize forced by a large
batch returned in 5-9 ms and close() in 10 ms; monerod stopped over RPC or
by SIGINT four seconds into it exited 1.1 s later with rc 0.

core::init calls it; see the next commit for where.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
